{% extends "mapper.j2" %}

{% block project_guidance -%}
You are working on the tests for the mngr_claude plugin (`libs/mngr_claude/`),
which wraps Claude Code sessions as mngr agents. The behaviors in this corpus
are observable only by driving a REAL Claude Code process, so their witnesses
are end-to-end release tests:

- Follow the existing pattern in
  `imbue/mngr_claude/test_claude_agent_e2e.py` (see
  `test_claude_bang_prefix_does_not_strand_agent_in_shell_mode`): marks
  `@pytest.mark.release` + `@pytest.mark.tmux` + `@pytest.mark.rsync` +
  `@pytest.mark.timeout(1500)`, a profile class with an
  `unavailable_reason()` guard that skips cleanly when the host cannot run
  the test, and pane-capture/poll helpers for observing the agent.
- A witness must assert what a CLIENT observes -- the conversation content
  and the agent's readiness for the next message -- never mngr's terminal
  internals. Readiness is observed via the `❯` input prompt, as the existing
  helpers do.
- Pure in-process logic (message classification, evidence probes) belongs in
  the colocated `*_test.py` unit tests next to the module under test; only
  agent-observable behavior goes in the e2e file.
- Read `libs/mngr_claude/imbue/mngr_claude/conftest.py` and the surrounding
  tests before writing new setup; reuse their fixtures and idioms. Never use
  unittest.mock or monkeypatch.setattr.
{%- endblock %}

{% block infra_blockers -%}
These witnesses need a real `claude` CLI on PATH, tmux, and rsync on the
host, plus working Claude credentials. Each e2e test may take many minutes
(the pattern allows up to 1500s); run only the node ids you touch, and prefer
running them one at a time rather than under xdist parallelism. If `claude`
or its credentials are absent from THIS environment, record the blocker
instead of faking the agent. Never stand up substitutes for Claude Code
itself -- a witness that does not drive a real Claude Code process is not a
witness of this corpus's behavior.
{%- endblock %}
